    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The available design and performance data for fractured wells is contradictory and confusing. Furthermore, recent well testing methods often indicate the result of fracturing is not the desired result.The main purpose of this study was to establish unique and correct fractured well performance data by relating potentiometric and computed results.Assuming a well connected with a vertical fracture is in the center of a drainage system with a constant pressure outer boundary, it is desired to determine the well productivity in terms of reservoir and fracture parameters. The effectiveness of a fracture to increase the well productivity depends upon the fracture length, width, height, permeability to flowing fluid, and position in the reservoir.Based upon the analogy of fluid flow in porous media to current flow in conducting materials, the major part of the problem solving techniques was by the potentiometric method. A potentiometric model was constructed as a 48~inch\"tank containing an electrolytic solution, where the fracture and reservoir conductivity ratio was approached by altering both the electrolyte concentration and fracture materials. The polarization resistance at the electrodes was eliminated by using high frequency alternating current. In addition, the intermediate voltages in the tank were measured and interpreted by a computer contouring program.Results of studies of the productivity of vertically-fractured wells obtained from the potentiometric model were presented as well flow efficiency, fracture pseudo skin factors, and effective well radii. It was found that the best presentation was the effective well radius. A single graph could do the work of a series of graphs commonly used to design fractures. This is the most important contribution of this study.Studies were also made from a steady-state finite-element computer model. It is believed that reasonable results were obtained from this numerical model.For high and medium conductivity fractures, results of this study, from both the potentiometric model and the finite-element model, were in excellent agreement with Prats analytical solutions (1961) and recently published data from transient studies.For low conductivity fractures, results from the potentiometric model and the finite-element model fell between Prats analytical solutions (1961) and the Tinsley, et al., electrolytic model studies (1969), but differed slightly from both.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
